  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry weather, slightly below average \ntemperatures, and lower humidity to end the week. \n\nThe well defined upper low continues to spin over NY/CT/MA early \nthis morning. The system has become nearly vertically stacked, with \nsfc low pressure close to Long Island. Isolated showers/tstms have \nexited NE NC, with light NW winds and milder temps in the mid 60s-\nlower 70s across the FA. The low pressure system will gradually \ntrack NE today and Friday, with dry LLVL NW flow continuing. This \nwill lead to lower humidity along with slightly below average temps. \nIn fact, afternoon dew pts could drop into the 50s inland today and \ntomorrow with highs generally in the mid to upper 80s. Overall, \nquite pleasant for late July. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A typical summertime pattern returns by late this \nweekend and early next week, with near average temperatures and \nincreasing shower/storm chances starting Sunday and continuing \nthrough early next week.\n\nUpper heights gradually build from Friday-Saturday with temps \nwarming back up to near average and mostly dry wx continuing. A \nweaker trough (more typical of summertime) drops SSE across the \nMississippi River Valle from Sat-Sat night and then slowly \napproaches the area on Sunday/Monday. At the same time, a weak cold \nfront approaches from the north, but will remain north of the local \narea through Monday (and likely wash out by Tue). With the \napproaching trough and Bermuda high pressure well offshore, deep-\nlayered S to SW flow will prevail. This will allow rich Gulf \nmoisture to overspread the area on Sunday and remain over us early \nnext week. PWs surge from a comfortable 1-1.4\" on Saturday to 2.2-\n2.4\" by late Sunday/Monday. Scattered to potentially numerous \nshowers/tstms are expected Sunday (with the highest chances inland). \nThen, showers/storms are likely across the entire area on Monday. \nThe highest storm coverage will be during the afternoon and evening \neach day. Cloud cover will keep high temps in the mid 80s on Sunday \nand lower-mid 80s Monday. Given the high PWs, weak flow aloft, and \ntall, skinny CAPE...not really looking at much in the way of severe \nwx but there will be a threat of localized flash flooding each day. \nThe unsettled pattern continues through at least Tuesday.",
